oleaut32: Keep a released type library read from a file for a while.

A type library read from a file stays in the typelib cache after its last
Release (up to 8 of them, the least recently released goes first), and
LoadTypeLib of the same, unchanged file (size and last write time) uses it
again instead of parsing the file again. A changed or deleted file is read
anew (or not found), also when GetRefTypeInfo looks for an imported library
in the cache. A kept library holds no references: the libraries it imports
are released and loaded again when needed, so their refcounts are the same
as on Windows.

Why: Inventor's 3D PDF export (Anark's corewrapper.exe) reads the part
through Inventor's COM API from another process. Every new object gets a
typelib-marshaled proxy and stub; get_typeinfo_for_iid() loads the type
library, takes the ITypeInfo and releases the library again, and the proxy
keeps no reference (Windows 10 neither, measured). So RxInventor.tlb went
out of the cache each time and was parsed again - about 0.5 s per load,
two per object, ~7 minutes for one export. With this: 15 s.

Windows 10 drops a library after its last Release too (another pointer
after reloading, in 2 of 3 measured runs), but loads it ~10x faster
(0.35-2.5 ms for a 2.2 MB library, Wine ~20 ms: it parses everything up
front). Measured with docs/wine-repro/oleaut32-typelib-cache/measure.c:
Windows and Wine show the same reference counts (no extra reference held
while a proxy and stub exist, ITypeLib refcount 0 after the last Release),
which this keeps.

The test checks what Windows does and what must stay correct with the cache:
reloading after the last Release, refcounts, and that another library
written to the same file is read anew. It passes without the change too;
the speed-up is shown by the MEASURE program (L3).

Written with AI help (Claude); not submitted to Wine. LGPL 2.1 or later.

